    ],
    "conclusion": "3 of 4 natural gate designs are evaded by an attacker who only slows down or re-instantiates. The two time-based gates fail under dilation because their state variable is a clock; the counter fails under re-instantiation because its state variable is session-scoped. The drift bound holds because its state variable is the POLICY STATE \u2014 the very quantity the attacker must move \u2014 so there is no gate memory left to attack.",
    "answers_A5": "Does the defense follow directly from the problem statement? It does not: the three designs that DO follow directly all fail here. What matters is not that a bound exists but WHERE its state variable lives, and that is the non-obvious part.",
    "answers_D_Q1": "This experiment targets time dilation specifically. Exceed-rate and maximum AMBR are reported per gate at 1x, 10x and 100x above."
